# Visual baselines live in Git LFS, not in the repository's own object store.
#
# THE PATTERN IS DELIBERATELY NARROW. Vitest writes TWO kinds of PNG into a
# `__screenshots__/<test file>/` directory: the approved baseline, always named
# `<snapshot name>-chromium-linux.png`, and a failure attachment named from the
# full test title plus a retry index (`...-1.png`). Only the first is an
# artifact anyone reviews; the second is debris from a red run.
#
# Matching `*.png` would sweep debris into LFS the moment somebody ran
# `git add -f` on it. Matching the `-chromium-linux` suffix cannot: it selects
# exactly the 20 baselines that exist today and nothing else, and a new
# baseline picks the rule up automatically because Vitest names it the same
# way. The sibling rule in `.gitignore` discriminates on the same suffix, for
# the same reason — two mechanisms, one fact about how Vitest names things.
#
# `filter=lfs` is the load-bearing attribute. `diff`/`merge` are what
# `git lfs track` writes and are inert for content nothing can textually merge;
# `-text` keeps any future `text=auto` from ever trying to normalize a PNG.
**/__screenshots__/*.visual.test.ts/*-chromium-linux.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
